# USER.md

Context on Jorge, the person Mr Fool works for, and how he approaches ASX micro-cap and small-cap investing. Soul.md defines who Mr Fool is, AGENTS.md defines the rules every agent in this workflow follows, this file is about the person on the other end.

## Background

- Based in Melbourne, Australia.
- Strong general analytical and quantitative background (engineering, machine learning, data analysis), but still building specific comfort with reading financial statements and ASX filings. Mr Fool should explain filing-specific reasoning as it goes (why a related-party note matters, what a going-concern flag implies) rather than assuming that literacy is already there, even though it can otherwise treat Jorge as an analytical peer.

## Investment goals

- Long-term growth and wealth building, not short-term trading.
- Micro-cap and small-cap ASX positions are a large focus of the overall portfolio (30%+ of total allocation).

## Sector focus

- Primary interest: technology, healthcare and biotech, and (tentatively) services and retail.
- Energy: renewables only, not fossil fuel exploration.
- Also interested in "picks and shovels" plays: companies supplying equipment or services into a boom (mining services, renewables infrastructure) rather than the speculative resource explorers themselves.
- Excluded: gambling, tobacco, and weapons (ethical screen), and pure exploration/mining plays (too commodity-driven, no operating cash flow to analyse).

## Position sizing and known blind spots

- Builds a small starter position and adds as the thesis confirms, rather than sizing up fully at once.
- Slow to cut losing positions. Mr Fool should be willing to say plainly when a thesis has broken down, not just when asked.
- Working on controlling FOMO-driven buying.
- Has previously been forced to sell shares that should have been left to run, because of not holding a cash buffer elsewhere (for example, needing cash on hand for an opportunistic buy in another asset). When a sell is being considered, Mr Fool should help distinguish "the thesis broke" from "I need liquidity for something unrelated," and can note the cash-buffer pattern if it looks relevant.

## Reporting and comparisons

- Default cadence: daily digest of watchlist announcements.
- When discussing relative valuation or performance, compare against both the ASX Small Ordinaries index and direct sector peers.

## Screening thresholds

- No fixed thresholds set yet (minimum market cap, cash runway, daily liquidity). Revisit once Mr Fool has visibility into the current portfolio and can help calibrate sensible cutoffs.

## Current holdings and watchlist

- Not yet provided. To be added once Jorge shares his current portfolio and watchlist, this will also help sharpen the screening thresholds above.
